CAPS — permission tokens

File: openOODA/CAPS.oot
Read this when code touches files, net, exec, hardware, or UI.
Real ops live in ../ooda/bootstrap/CAPS_MATRIX.oot
The compiler accepts the names below. A name is not the same as a sealed op.
FfiCap and UnsafeFFICap are the same token.

Rule
  Give only the token the function needs.
  Check at compile time. Re-check a process-local seal at run time.
  Missing or forged tokens fail closed.

Sealed today
  FsReadCap — read_file, path_exists, file_size
  FsWriteCap — write_file. Writes also obey OODA_FS_WRITEDIR
  FsCap — old broad file token. Still accepted. Prefer read or write.
  HttpCap — fetch
  TcpCap — tcp connect, read, write, close, tls_connect
  UdpCap — bind_udp, udp_recv. DNS resolve_ipv4 takes UdpCap. Real socket DNS is later.
  BindCap — tcp_bind
  NetCap — old broad net token. Required for sock_raw
  ProcessCap — sys_exec, spawn, wait, kill
  SysCap — old broad OS token. Still used for python_embed, epoll, inotify, prctl, async join
  EnvCap — env get. Keys OODA_ and OO_
  TimeCap — now, sleep
  RandCap — random
  AllocCap — heap
  ThreadCap — mutex, spawn, join, channels
  GpuCap — gpu_launch (CPU / noop path)
  FfiCap — C bindings (UnsafeFFICap is the same token)

Name accepted, product op later
  MemCap, SyncCap
  UsbCap, AudioCap, CameraCap, HidCap
  WindowCap, FrameCap
  AuditCap, SignCap, HitlCap

Not a cap
  SECRET: is taint, not a token

New code uses the small token that matches the op.
Old broad tokens still work where listed above.

Also see
  ../ooda/bootstrap/CAPS_MATRIX.oot — op to cap to runtime
  ../ooda/bootstrap/STATIC_CAPS.oot — process-local seal
  ../ooda/bootstrap/CAP_FFI.oot — FFI rules
  SHIPPED.oot — what you can run
